WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.562
The request is gone.

2
00:00:01.812 --> 00:00:02.891
Its pointer isn't.

3
00:00:03.141 --> 00:00:07.072
Inside a PHP worker, one request
creates data in

4
00:00:07.072 --> 00:00:07.508
memory.

5
00:00:07.758 --> 00:00:10.539
Worker-wide state keeps a
reference to that data.

6
00:00:10.789 --> 00:00:13.751
When the request ends, its
memory is released, but

7
00:00:13.751 --> 00:00:15.727
some state can still point
there.

8
00:00:15.977 --> 00:00:18.656
Now the same worker handles
another request.

9
00:00:18.906 --> 00:00:22.488
Before normal initialization
resets that state, an early

10
00:00:22.488 --> 00:00:25.174
operation can use the stale
reference.

11
00:00:25.724 --> 00:00:28.750
That's use-after-free: accessing
memory after its

12
00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:30.263
lifetime has ended.

13
00:00:30.513 --> 00:00:34.074
Under exploitable conditions, an
attacker can turn that

14
00:00:34.074 --> 00:00:36.745
memory error into remote code
execution.

15
00:00:36.995 --> 00:00:40.379
That means the worker's
permissions, not automatic root

16
00:00:40.379 --> 00:00:40.802
access.

17
00:00:41.052 --> 00:00:44.747
The exact trigger depends on the
deployment and internal

18
00:00:44.747 --> 00:00:45.568
state involved.

19
00:00:45.818 --> 00:00:48.949
PHP's fix addresses this
lifecycle bug.

20
00:00:49.199 --> 00:00:53.227
Patch the runtime, restart
affected workers, and verify

21
00:00:53.227 --> 00:00:55.744
the version actually serving
requests.
